 "parts": [
  "The shopping cart is usually credited to a grocer in Oklahoma City.",
  "Nineteen thirty-seven.",
  "And at first, nobody wanted it.",
  "Sylvan Goldman ran a chain of grocery stores.",
  "According to NPR, he noticed shoppers stopped buying when their arms were full.",
  "His fix: a folding frame holding two wire baskets.",
  "He called his campaign the No Basket Carrying Plan.",
  "Men thought it looked effeminate.",
  "Women said it looked like pushing a baby carriage.",
  "So he hired models to shop with the carts.",
  "And greeters to show people how to use them.",
  "In nineteen seventy-seven, he was asked if customers loved the cart.",
  "His answer, as NPR reports it: No, no. That's a big lie.",
  "Then it caught on.",
  "A rival design nested one cart inside another.",
  "Orla Watson filed it in nineteen forty-six. The patent came in nineteen forty-nine.",
  "Sometimes you have to hire the crowd."
 ],
